{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Akalū</b> — this verse corresponds to 'the humble shall eat and be satisfied' (Psalms 22:27), for the fat ones of the earth are those who take pleasure in this world, to eat every fatness." "<b>And the fat ones of the earth</b> — the opposite of 'the humble.' The meaning: even if they indulge in this world, in the end and in their latter days they shall bow down to the King who gathers their spirit, and they have no strength to rebel. And likewise, 'before Him shall bow all who go down to the dust' (Psalms 22:30)." "And the meaning of 'and his soul did not live' — after the manner of 'the daughters of Step-ah upon my ox' [a construction where one of a pair cannot sustain its soul]. For not one of them can keep his soul alive, and this is a hint that their soul shall perish in this world, the opposite of the humble, concerning whom it is written: 'your heart shall live forever' (Psalms 22:27)."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {2 "The phrase 'בנות צעדה עלי שור' is textually obscure; translated hyper-literally as it stands. It may be corrupt or a cryptic grammatical allusion Ibn Ezra assumes the reader will recognize."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:28:22.013913966Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}